1068 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 318 0 R
/E (The proposed system integrates retail transaction simulation with digital forensic monitoring to create a secure environment capable of identifying suspicious activities and integrity violations efficiently. The system combines file monitoring, cryptographic hashing, dashboard visualization, attack simulation, and report generation into a single cybersecurity platform designed for retail breach analysis and forensic investigation)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1069 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 318 0 R
/E (2.1 Existing System)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1070 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 318 0 R
/E (The existing retail systems used in many organizations are primarily designed to manage sales operations, billing processes, inventory records, and customer transactions. These systems focus mainly on improving business efficiency, transaction speed, and operational management. However, most traditional retail systems provide very limited cybersecurity monitoring and forensic investigation capabilities.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1071 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 318 0 R
/E (In many retail environments, transaction records and activity logs are stored locally or within cloud storage systems without continuous integrity verification. If an attacker gains unauthorized access to the system and modifies transaction data, deletes activity logs, or injects malicious records, the changes may remain undetected for long periods of time. This creates serious security risks and increases the difficulty of identifying cyber incidents during forensic investigations.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1072 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 318 0 R
/E (Traditional retail systems mainly depend on manual monitoring and administrator-based investigation methods. Administrators usually review logs and transaction records only after suspicious activities are reported. Manual forensic investigation consumes significant time and may delay incident response processes. In many cases, attackers modify important files or remove evidence before the organization identifies the breach. Most existing systems also lack automated integrity verification mechanisms such as cryptographic hashing. Without integrity verification, organizations cannot easily determine whether transaction records or operational files have been modified without authorization. Attackers may manipulate billing values, alter customer transaction histories, or inject fraudulent entries into activity logs without generating immediate alerts.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1073 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 318 0 R
/E (Another limitation of traditional retail infrastructures is the absence of centralized dashboard-based monitoring systems. Security-related information is often scattered across multiple logs and files, making it difficult for investigators to analyze incidents efficiently. The lack of real-time monitoring and automated threat detection reduces overall cybersecurity visibility within retail environments.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
